    <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\displaystyle \operatorname {Ind} _{H}^{G}\pi =\left\{\phi \colon G\to V\</annotation></semantics></math><span typeof="mw:DisplaySpace"> </span>:\ \phi (gh^{-1})=\Delta _{G}^{-{\frac {1}{2}}}(h)\Delta _{H}^{\frac {1}{2}}(h)\pi (h)\phi (g){\text{ and }}\phi \in L^{2}(G/H)\right\}.}
  
</span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/1397abf7831bc66e9b427ba7eefae572b5728cb9" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -2.505ex; width:80.624ex; height:6.176ex;" alt="{\displaystyle \operatorname {Ind} _{H}^{G}\pi =\left\{\phi \colon G\to V\ :\ \phi (gh^{-1})=\Delta _{G}^{-{\frac {1}{2}}}(h)\Delta _{H}^{\frac {1}{2}}(h)\pi (h)\phi (g){\text{ and }}\phi \in L^{2}(G/H)\right\}.}"/></span></dd></dl>

<p id="mw1A">Here <span class="texhtml " about="#mwt100" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw1Q" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"math","href":"./Template:Math"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Δ&lt;sub>&apos;&apos;G&apos;&apos;&lt;/sub>, Δ&lt;sub>&apos;&apos;H&apos;&apos;&lt;/sub>"}},"i":0}}]}'>Δ<sub><i>G</i></sub>, Δ<sub><i>H</i></sub></span> are the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haar_measure#The_modular_function" title="Haar measure" id="mw1g">modular functions</a> of <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt101" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw1w" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"G"}},"i":0}}]}'>G</span> and <span class="texhtml mvar" style="font-style:italic;" about="#mwt102" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mw2A" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mvar","href":"./Template:Mvar"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"H"}},"i":0}}]}'>H</span> respectively.  With the addition of the <i id="mw2Q">normalizing</i> factors this induction <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Functor" title="Functor" id="mw2g">functor</a> takes <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unitary_representation" title="Unitary representation" id="mw2w">unitary representations</a> to unitary representations.</p>

<p id="mw3A">One other variation on induction is called <b id="mw3Q">compact induction</b>.  This is just standard induction restricted to functions with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_support" title="Compact support" class="mw-redirect" id="mw3g">compact support</a>. Formally it is denoted by ind and defined as:</p>

<dl id="mw3w"><dd id="mw4A"><span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt103" id="mw4Q" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"\\operatorname{ind}_H^G\\pi= \\left\\{\\phi\\colon G \\to V \\ : \\ \\phi(gh^{-1})=\\pi(h)\\phi(g) \\text{ and } \\phi \\text{ has compact support mod } H \\right\\}."}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle \operatorname {ind} _{H}^{G}\pi =\left\{\phi \colon G\to V\ :\ \phi (gh^{-1})=\pi (h)\phi (g){\text{ and }}\phi {\text{ has compact support mod }}H\right\}.}">
  <semantics>
